<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #e0f2f7; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Recycling for Our World – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences. Reading: Identify themes in simple stories. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend short narratives about recycling by connecting them with personal experiences (e.g., "I recycle bottles like in the story."). Can identify themes in simple recycling-related stories and provide examples or connections from the story (e.g., "The story is about saving the Earth because the characters recycle bottles and plant trees."). Can segment and decode multisyllabic words into syllables (e.g., dividing "environment" into "en-vi-ron-ment").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students a large picture of a recycling bin full of *plastic bottles*. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "What can we do with these *plastic bottles*?" The teacher writes key words like "recycle," "bottles," "plastic," and "bin" on the board. Students quickly match these words to the picture by pointing or raising hands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple storybook or a printed text with the title "Our Green School." The teacher models how to look at the title and pictures to predict what the story is about. For example, "This story is about a school. I see a green bin. Maybe it is about *recycling* at school." The teacher models pronouncing "/r/" in "recycle" and "/s/" in "sustainability" clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary from the previous lesson and the theme. "Is a *plastic bottle* something we throw in the regular *trash*? (No, we *recycle* it.)" "If we *recycle*, do we *help* the *environment* or *pollute* it? (We *help* the *environment*.)"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt titled "Lily's Recycling Day." The text is printed with clear illustrations and short sentences, incorporating vocabulary like *plastic bottles*, *recycle*, *bin*, *environment*, *save*, *easy*, *often*, *there is/are*, *can*. The genre is a simple narrative, focusing on familiar experiences at an A1.3 level.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Text Example:</i><br>
                "Lily *often* helps her family. Every morning, *there is* a blue *bin* for *plastic bottles*. Lily *can* *recycle* many things. Her brother Tom helps too. They *save* the *environment*. It *is* *easy* to *recycle*!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehension of short, simple written texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-reading (Gist):</b> The teacher writes "Lily's Recycling Day" on the board and shows pictures related to the story (e.g., a girl, a blue bin, plastic bottles). Students look at the title and pictures. The teacher asks, "What do you think this story is about?" (e.g., "A girl and *recycling*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Reading (Listen & Follow):</b> The teacher reads the narrative aloud clearly while students follow along in their own copies.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> After the first reading, the teacher asks general questions: "Who helps with *recycling* in the story? (Lily and Tom.)" "What do they *recycle*? (*Plastic bottles*.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read statements and say if they are true or false based on the story. Example: "Lily throws *plastic bottles* in the *trash*. (False)" "It is *easy* to *recycle*. (True)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying target vocabulary, grammar (present simple, can/could, there is/are), and phonemic awareness (segmenting multisyllabic words related to sustainability).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary Spotlight & Underlining:</b> Students reread "Lily's Recycling Day." The teacher asks them to find and underline all the words from a provided list (e.g., *recycle*, *plastic*, *bottles*, *bin*, *save*, *environment*, *easy*, *often*). Students compare their underlined words with a partner.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion & Grammar Focus:</b> The teacher provides sentences from the story with missing words (a simple cloze activity). Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using words from a word bank. This reinforces present simple, *can*, and *there is/are*. Example: "Every morning, _____ a blue bin for plastic bottles." (Answer: *there is*). "Lily _____ recycle many things." (Answer: *can*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Theme Identification & Phonemic Awareness:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read three short sentences related to the story's theme. Example: A) "Recycling *plastic bottles* helps the *Earth*." B) "Lily and Tom *save* the *environment*." C) "It's important to *recycle* for *sustainability*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What is the main idea of these sentences? What are they all about?" (e.g., "*Saving* the *environment* by *recycling*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then focuses on multisyllabic words: "Let's read the word '*environment*'. How many parts? 'en-vi-ron-ment'. (4 syllables)" "Now '*sustainability*'. 'sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty'. (6 syllables)" Students practice segmenting these words by clapping or tapping.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Reading Response and Connection.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a short, simple new text (2-3 sentences) about *recycling* in a different context (e.g., "Our School Recycling Program"). This text uses similar vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Working in pairs, students read the new text silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they collaboratively answer two comprehension questions related to the text and one question connecting it to their own lives, encouraging them to use the target vocabulary and grammar orally based on their reading:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *can* we *recycle* at school, according to the text?" (e.g., "We *can* *recycle* *paper* and *plastic bottles*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why *is* the *recycling program* important?" (e.g., "It *is* important to *save* our *environment*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you *recycle plastic bottles* at home? How *often*?" (e.g., "Yes, I *often* *recycle* *plastic bottles* at home.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair shares one answer with the class, focusing on clear reading and understanding of the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Short Quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a very short, new story (2-3 sentences) about a child *recycling* at home. They read it independently and then complete a 3-question multiple-choice quiz or fill-in-the-blank activity to check comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Quiz Questions:</i></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does the child *recycle* in the story? (a) food scraps (b) *plastic bottles* (c) paper</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Where *is* the *recycling bin*? (a) in the kitchen (b) outside (c) in the bedroom</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The story *is* about: (a) playing games (b) *saving* the *environment* (c) cooking food</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in answering comprehension questions (identifies main idea, key details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of themes in simple stories.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in decoding new words and connecting text to personal experience.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete these sentences:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">1. Today I learned about __________________________ from reading the story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2. I *can* read stories about *recycling* and understand __________________________.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">1. What part of reading the stories about *recycling* was *easy* for me today?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2. What part was difficult, and how *can* I improve my reading next time?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students successfully identified key vocabulary and general themes in the recycling stories. The phonemic awareness activity for "environment" and "sustainability" was engaging, but some students struggled with segmenting longer words. I need to provide more visual aids or rhythmic activities for multisyllabic words in future lessons. The connection to personal experiences was strong and helped motivate students. The pair work encouraged collaborative reading and discussion about the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and understanding gained from these reading activities to actively speak and interact about *recycling plastic bottles* and *saving* the *environment* in pairs and small groups. They will practice asking and answering questions like "What *can* we *recycle*?" and giving reasons why "We *recycle* to *help* the *planet*."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>